<header><p class="enc-kicker">Event</p><h1 class="enc-title">Second Stadtholderless Period</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1702">1702</a>–<a href="/years/1747">1747</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Second Stadtholderless Period was the era from 1702 to 1747 when most provinces of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic</a> left the office of stadtholder vacant after the death of William III. Regent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oligarchy">oligarchies</a> governed under grand pensionaries such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthonie-heinsius">Anthonie Heinsius</a>, while the costs of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> burdened the Republic with debt. It ended when a French invasion in 1747 provoked an Orangist revolt and William IV became hereditary stadtholder of all the provinces.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/second-stadtholderless-period.jpg" alt="Second Stadtholderless Period" width="251" height="357" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: P. van den Berge · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Fall_of_William_III.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>The period followed an unusually strong stadtholderate. A popular revolt against the French invasion of 1672 had overturned the States-Party regime of Grand Pensionary Johan de Witt and brought <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-iii-of-england">William III of Orange</a> to power in Holland and Zeeland. He was allowed to purge the Holland city governments of States-Party regents, the office was made hereditary in his male line in 1674, and Utrecht, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gelderland">Gelderland</a> and Overijssel had to accept regulations that let him appoint most of their officials. Childless, William named <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-william-friso">John William Friso</a>, head of the Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diez">Dietz</a> branch, his general heir before his death on 19 March 1702.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>With the office vacant in Holland, Zeeland and Utrecht, the States Party returned to power, though members of the House of Nassau-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diez">Dietz</a> were stadtholders of other provinces at various times. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthonie-heinsius">Anthonie Heinsius</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/grand-pensionary">Grand Pensionary</a> since 1689, kept the Republic in the grand coalition against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xiv">Louis XIV</a>, providing troops and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/subsidy">subsidies</a>, but at the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-spanish-succession">War of the Spanish Succession</a> it was financially exhausted. After Heinsius died in August 1720 the office passed to Isaac van Hoornbeek, then Simon van Slingelandt, and in 1736 to Anthonie van der Heim, who promised in writing to oppose any revival of the stadtholderate.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> historians call these years the Pruikentijd, the periwig era, a scornful name for a time of political and military decline whose main cause was economic. High Dutch wages made exports uncompetitive once prices fell after about 1670, and protectionism in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and, after 1720, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scandinavia">Scandinavia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> closed markets. The maritime provinces lost industry and population, while a rentier class built up the capital that later gave the Republic its lead in international finance.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>The Republic drifted into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/war-of-the-austrian-succession">War of the Austrian Succession</a>. After the defeat at the Battle of Fontenoy in 1745 the French overran the Austrian Netherlands and in April 1747 occupied <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/zeelandic-flanders">States Flanders</a>. Recalling 1672, the people demanded the stadtholderate back, and an Orangist revolution spread from Zeeland to Holland and the other provinces. Restoration did not end the troubles: William V succeeded his father at the age of three, and the stadtholderate lasted until <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/french-revolutionary-occupation-of-the-netherlands">French revolutionary armies</a> drove him into exile in January 1795. The name of the period was fixed by 19th-century Orangist historians such as Guillaume Groen van Prinsterer, whose verdict later historians questioned.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Second Stadtholderless Period” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>A <b>Second Stadtholderless Period</b> followed William III’s death in 1702 and ended in 1747, when William IV became hereditary stadtholder of all the provinces.</blockquote><a href="/empire/dutch-republic">Dutch Republic · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
